Radix as a Go target (ADR 0350): GOOS=radix, a Unix, on amd64. The
compiler, assembler and linker treat it as OpenBSD where a program calls
the C library: a dynamically linked ELF program whose interpreter is
/system/libraries/ld.so and which needs libc.so, linked by Go's own
linker, position-independent by default; the goroutine pointer in
thread-local storage at -8(FS), reached through initial exec in
position-independent code; and the larger stack guard that C library
calls need.

cgo is supported (ADR 0357). A program with C code is linked by the C
toolchain's linker, since Go's linker links only Go code to the C
library; the C threads of cgo's own tests are built for Radix too. Go's
linker builds its output in memory and writes it at the end, because
Radix refuses a shared writable mapping of a file. The go command locks
files with flock, and its local telemetry counters record no kernel
version. Dukovo only.

